<section class="wrap section topic" id="power">
  <h2>Power</h2>
  <p>A data center runs around the clock. <a class="cite" href="https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=28232" rel="noopener">[EIA]</a> This one likely uses more electricity than every home in South Brunswick combined.<sup><a href="#m-homes" id="r-homes">1</a></sup></p>
  <p>Its 12 backup generators total about 43 megawatts.<sup><a href="#m-capacity" id="r-capacity">2</a></sup> For scale, every home in South Brunswick together uses about 15 megawatts on average. Our assumption, a reasonable one for a data center, is that the backup is sized to carry the whole facility. If so, the 43 megawatts is a clue to how much power the data center itself draws, every hour of every day.<sup><a href="#m-homes">1</a></sup></p>
  <p>New Jersey's Data Center Fair Share law requires data centers to cut their grid use when the grid is strained. <a class="cite" href="https://www.nj.gov/governor/news/2026/20260707a.shtml" rel="noopener">[Governor's office]</a> Unless NJDEP restricts it, the easiest way to do that is to switch on the diesel generators. See <a href="#air">Air</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap section topic" id="noise">
  <h2>Noise</h2>
  <p>Data centers run around the clock, and so does their cooling equipment. <a class="cite" href="https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=28232" rel="noopener">[EIA]</a> On top of that, the draft permit allows routine testing of the 12 diesel engines: one at a time between 8 a.m. and 8 p.m., and up to six at once between noon and 4 p.m.<sup><a href="#m-testing" id="r-testing">3</a></sup></p>
  <p>Large engines like these make a deep hum, with strong tones near 30 and 300 cycles per second.<sup><a href="#m-tones" id="r-tones">4</a></sup> Low-frequency noise from large cooling systems travels farther and passes through walls and windows with little loss. One researcher estimates it would take a wall about 4.5 feet thick to block a 63 Hz hum. <a class="cite" href="https://www.facilitiesdive.com/news/45-foot-thick-walls-needed-to-absorb-data-center-noise-researcher/831843/" rel="noopener">[Facilities Dive]</a> New Jersey's noise rule sets separate limits for low-frequency bands (<a href="{{ site.laws.noise.url }}" rel="noopener">{{ site.laws.noise.cite }}</a>), but no one has shown whether the developer's noise study measured them.</p>
  {% for k in c.concerns %}{% if k.area == "noise" %}{% set s = c | sourceById(k.source) %}
  <p>{{ k.text }} <a class="cite" href="{{ s.url }}" rel="noopener">[{{ s.publisher }}]</a></p>
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  <p class="muted small">We're asking for the noise study. <a href="/documents/">See the records list</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap section topic" id="air">
  <div class="callout">
    <h2>Air: a diesel power plant in our backyard</h2>
    <p>The data center is seeking a state permit for 12 diesel generators, about 43 megawatts of capacity.<sup><a href="#m-capacity">2</a></sup> When all 12 run, the draft permit allows them to emit up to 900 pounds of nitrogen oxides (NOx) an hour.<sup><a href="#m-nox" id="r-nox">5</a></sup> That's as much as a 600-megawatt coal plant, or about 14 times the average coal plant per unit of electricity.<sup><a href="#m-coal" id="r-coal">6</a></sup></p>
    <p>NOx forms smog and worsens asthma, especially in children and older adults. Diesel exhaust causes cancer.<sup><a href="#m-health" id="r-health">7</a></sup></p>
    <p>Sentinel will say the generators rarely run, but only testing is capped. Emergency use has no hourly limit, and the permit lets them run whenever the regional grid is strained, often on the hottest, smoggiest days.<sup><a href="#m-hours" id="r-hours">8</a></sup> NOAA expects one of the strongest El Niños on record this winter, and NASA says the last strong El Niño helped make 2024 the warmest year on record.<sup><a href="#m-elnino" id="r-elnino">9</a></sup> Nothing in the permit requires monitoring what comes out of these stacks.<sup><a href="#m-monitor" id="r-monitor">10</a></sup></p>
    <p class="cta" data-until="2026-11-09"><strong>Tell NJDEP what you think by November 9.</strong> <a href="/comment/">How to comment</a><sup><a href="#m-deadline" id="r-deadline">11</a></sup></p>
  </div>
</section>

